1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,516
A scraped knee.

2
00:00:01,516 --> 00:00:02,582
A growing baby.

3
00:00:02,582 --> 00:00:04,389
An onion root getting longer.

4
00:00:04,789 --> 00:00:07,330
Diya scraped her knee
on the college steps.

5
00:00:07,330 --> 00:00:10,727
One week later, new skin had
covered the wound completely.

6
00:00:11,177 --> 00:00:15,425
She wants to be a doctor, and she asked:
where did all those new skin cells

7
00:00:15,425 --> 00:00:16,325
come from?

8
00:00:16,520 --> 00:00:19,594
Every new cell comes from
an old cell that divided.

9
00:00:19,594 --> 00:00:22,577
But first, the cell must
grow and copy its DNA.

10
00:00:22,577 --> 00:00:24,855
That whole journey is the cell cycle.

11
00:00:25,355 --> 00:00:29,463
By the end, you will name every phase
of the cell cycle, in order, and

12
00:00:29,463 --> 00:00:31,245
say what happens in each one.

13
00:00:31,895 --> 00:00:33,784
First, what is a cell cycle?

14
00:00:34,134 --> 00:00:37,509
It is the set of steps by which a
cell copies its DNA, makes its other

15
00:00:37,509 --> 00:00:39,984
parts, and finally divides
into two daughter cells.

16
00:00:40,434 --> 00:00:43,106
Daughter cells just
means the two new cells.

17
00:00:43,106 --> 00:00:46,105
Each daughter can then
start the same cycle again.

18
00:00:46,555 --> 00:00:48,472
How long does one turn take?

19
00:00:48,472 --> 00:00:52,842
Human cells growing in a lab divide
about once every twenty four hours.

20
00:00:52,842 --> 00:00:56,572
Yeast, a tiny fungus, can do
it in about ninety minutes.

21
00:00:57,222 --> 00:01:00,719
Let's zoom in, and find cells
in the middle of their cycle.

22
00:01:01,069 --> 00:01:03,228
An onion root grows at its tip.

23
00:01:03,228 --> 00:01:05,581
The tip is where cells divide fast.

24
00:01:06,081 --> 00:01:07,426
Zoom into the tip.

25
00:01:07,426 --> 00:01:10,309
Most cells look calm,
with a round nucleus.

26
00:01:10,309 --> 00:01:12,747
A few look busy, with dark threads.

27
00:01:13,247 --> 00:01:15,057
Zoom into a calm nucleus.

28
00:01:15,057 --> 00:01:16,454
It is not resting.

29
00:01:16,454 --> 00:01:18,576
It is getting ready to divide.

30
00:01:19,076 --> 00:01:21,887
Inside sits the DNA, the
instruction book of the cell.

31
00:01:21,887 --> 00:01:25,176
Before the cell divides, every
page of this book must be copied.

32
00:01:25,826 --> 00:01:29,486
Now let's run one full
cell cycle, phase by phase.

33
00:01:29,836 --> 00:01:30,736
Phase one.

34
00:01:30,661 --> 00:01:31,561
G one.

35
00:01:31,297 --> 00:01:35,851
The cell grows bigger and works hard,
but it does not copy its DNA yet.

36
00:01:35,851 --> 00:01:37,123
G stands for gap.

37
00:01:37,873 --> 00:01:38,773
Phase two.

38
00:01:38,645 --> 00:01:39,870
S, for synthesis.

39
00:01:39,870 --> 00:01:41,334
Now the DNA is copied.

40
00:01:41,334 --> 00:01:44,361
The amount of DNA doubles,
from two C to four C.

41
00:01:44,361 --> 00:01:47,095
In animal cells, the
centriole is copied too.

42
00:01:47,845 --> 00:01:48,745
Phase three.

43
00:01:48,600 --> 00:01:49,500
G two.

44
00:01:49,121 --> 00:01:52,638
The cell keeps growing, and makes
the proteins it needs for division.

45
00:01:53,388 --> 00:01:54,288
Phase four.

46
00:01:54,192 --> 00:01:55,310
M, for mitosis.

47
00:01:55,310 --> 00:01:59,214
The copied chromosomes are shared
out, and the cell splits into two.

48
00:01:59,964 --> 00:02:02,922
Then each daughter cell
can begin again at G one.

49
00:02:02,922 --> 00:02:05,056
That is why it is called a cycle.

50
00:02:05,806 --> 00:02:08,011
The whole cycle has two big parts.

51
00:02:08,361 --> 00:02:11,163
Interphase is G one, S and G two together.

52
00:02:11,163 --> 00:02:13,525
It is the time between two divisions.

53
00:02:14,025 --> 00:02:16,224
M phase is the division itself.

54
00:02:16,224 --> 00:02:21,264
In a human cell, out of twenty four
hours, it lasts only about one hour.

55
00:02:21,764 --> 00:02:25,711
So interphase takes more than
ninety five percent of the cycle.

56
00:02:25,711 --> 00:02:29,920
It was once called the resting
phase, but the cell is very busy.

57
00:02:30,420 --> 00:02:31,906
And some cells stop.

58
00:02:31,906 --> 00:02:36,386
Cells that do not divide further
leave the cycle from G one, and enter

59
00:02:36,386 --> 00:02:39,718
a quiet stage called G
zero, the quiescent stage.

60
00:02:39,718 --> 00:02:44,547
They stay alive and working, but divide
only when the body needs them to.

61
00:02:45,197 --> 00:02:47,290
Side by side, G one and G two.

62
00:02:47,290 --> 00:02:48,190
When.

63
00:02:47,793 --> 00:02:50,110
G one comes before the DNA is copied.

64
00:02:50,110 --> 00:02:51,547
G two comes after it.

65
00:02:52,297 --> 00:02:53,256
DNA amount.

66
00:02:53,256 --> 00:02:55,207
In G one, it is two C.

67
00:02:55,207 --> 00:02:57,793
In G two, it is four C, double.

68
00:02:58,543 --> 00:02:59,731
Chromosome number.

69
00:02:59,731 --> 00:03:01,376
In both, it is two n.

70
00:03:01,376 --> 00:03:05,500
Copying gives each chromosome two
copies, joined together, but it adds

71
00:03:05,500 --> 00:03:06,790
no new chromosomes.

72
00:03:07,540 --> 00:03:08,440
Your turn.

73
00:03:08,331 --> 00:03:10,442
A cell has just finished S phase.

74
00:03:10,442 --> 00:03:14,018
Which phase is it in now, and
how much DNA does it have?

75
00:03:14,018 --> 00:03:15,183
Pause and decide.

76
00:03:18,383 --> 00:03:19,944
G two, with four C!

77
00:03:19,944 --> 00:03:23,476
The DNA was copied in S
phase, so now it is double.

78
00:03:24,126 --> 00:03:25,109
Remember this.

79
00:03:25,109 --> 00:03:26,388
S doubles the DNA.

80
00:03:26,388 --> 00:03:28,558
It does not double the chromosomes.

81
00:03:29,158 --> 00:03:30,644
Now, a worked example.

82
00:03:30,644 --> 00:03:31,544
Six events.

83
00:03:31,453 --> 00:03:32,851
Interphase or M phase?

84
00:03:33,201 --> 00:03:34,422
The DNA is copied.

85
00:03:34,422 --> 00:03:35,950
Interphase, in S phase.

86
00:03:36,400 --> 00:03:38,762
The chromosomes are pulled apart.

87
00:03:38,762 --> 00:03:39,662
M phase.

88
00:03:40,038 --> 00:03:42,238
The cell grows, before copying its DNA.

89
00:03:42,238 --> 00:03:43,564
Interphase, in G one.

90
00:03:44,014 --> 00:03:46,719
The cell splits into two daughter cells.

91
00:03:46,719 --> 00:03:47,619
M phase.

92
00:03:47,948 --> 00:03:50,384
The nuclear envelope breaks down.

93
00:03:50,384 --> 00:03:51,284
M phase.

94
00:03:51,687 --> 00:03:53,943
And the proteins for division are made.

95
00:03:53,943 --> 00:03:55,356
Interphase, in G two.

96
00:03:56,006 --> 00:03:57,468
Now, the exam trap.

97
00:03:57,818 --> 00:04:01,575
Many students think S phase doubles
the number of chromosomes, because

98
00:04:01,575 --> 00:04:02,647
the DNA doubles.

99
00:04:03,047 --> 00:04:07,329
But the DNA goes from two C to four
C, while the chromosome number stays

100
00:04:07,329 --> 00:04:08,229
two n.

101
00:04:07,946 --> 00:04:11,104
Each chromosome now has two
copies, joined together.

102
00:04:11,754 --> 00:04:13,262
Where is this asked?

103
00:04:13,262 --> 00:04:18,180
As the order of the phases, the DNA
content in each phase, and the G zero

104
00:04:18,180 --> 00:04:19,080
stage.

105
00:04:19,397 --> 00:04:20,574
Exam level now.

106
00:04:20,574 --> 00:04:21,901
Read the question.

107
00:04:21,901 --> 00:04:23,127
Pause and solve.

108
00:04:28,527 --> 00:04:29,790
The answer is A.

109
00:04:30,290 --> 00:04:33,708
S phase copies the DNA, so
two C becomes four C by G two.

110
00:04:34,208 --> 00:04:38,110
But no new chromosomes are made,
so the number stays at eight.

111
00:04:38,710 --> 00:04:40,250
Here is your recap.

112
00:04:40,250 --> 00:04:41,833
Screenshot this card.

113
00:04:45,033 --> 00:04:47,219
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

114
00:04:47,619 --> 00:04:48,519
One.

115
00:04:48,144 --> 00:04:51,645
Name the four phases of
the cell cycle, in order.

116
00:04:52,545 --> 00:04:53,445
Two.

117
00:04:52,930 --> 00:04:54,799
What happens to the DNA in S phase?

118
00:04:55,699 --> 00:04:56,599
Three.

119
00:04:56,267 --> 00:04:57,996
What is the G zero stage?

120
00:04:59,096 --> 00:05:03,704
Next time: how does a cell pull its
copied chromosomes apart so neatly?

121
00:05:03,704 --> 00:05:06,786
We find out in mitosis,
prophase to telophase.
